1
00:00:03,685 --> 00:00:07,640
This time, let's have a Q&A session.

2
00:00:08,119 --> 00:00:17,160
Through the community post on my Class 101 class introduction page, I received questions separately, but there was only one question.

3
00:00:17,161 --> 00:00:19,800
You were all shy, weren't you?

4
00:00:19,801 --> 00:00:23,601
So I put together the questions I commonly see online.

5
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:30,120
It was the only question post in the community.

6
00:00:30,121 --> 00:00:39,240
I'm wondering whether you first think about the surrounding space and then place the figure inside that space, or whether you draw the figure first and then draw the surrounding space to fit it.

7
00:00:39,241 --> 00:00:42,709
Drawing to match the perspective is too tricky.

8
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:45,240
Gyeong-jeong asked this question.

9
00:00:45,241 --> 00:00:47,960
Well, this seems like a question about illustration.

10
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:51,720
This isn't an illustration class, but let me give a brief answer.

11
00:00:51,721 --> 00:00:56,220
I draw the figure first and then draw the surrounding space.

12
00:00:56,221 --> 00:00:59,020
That's because I'm a character illustrator.

13
00:00:59,021 --> 00:01:06,140
I first conceptualize a composition where the character is effectively well expressed, and then draw the surrounding space to match it.

14
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:09,621
If the background were the main subject, it would be the opposite.

15
00:01:11,024 --> 00:01:16,650
As you can see in this picture, the only thing around is a mirror.

16
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:18,810
Do you think I drew the mirror first?

17
00:01:18,811 --> 00:01:19,610
No.

18
00:01:19,611 --> 00:01:22,090
I drew the character first.

19
00:01:22,091 --> 00:01:25,971
The mirror was drawn later to match the character's perspective.

20
00:01:27,529 --> 00:01:28,890
I draw the perspective lines like this.

21
00:01:30,225 --> 00:01:32,170
Let me also show you the finished result.

22
00:01:32,171 --> 00:01:37,290
The window background behind this was also drawn based on the character.

23
00:01:37,291 --> 00:01:39,450
Likewise, after drawing the character.

24
00:01:40,809 --> 00:01:43,050
I find the perspective lines and draw them.

25
00:01:43,505 --> 00:01:47,411
Then you just express the surrounding environment to match those lines.

26
00:01:48,699 --> 00:01:52,990
Did that resolve your curiosity?

27
00:01:53,014 --> 00:01:58,500
Now the next question. I'm XX years old. Is it too late to start drawing?

28
00:01:59,689 --> 00:02:01,210
I've seen this kind of question a lot.

29
00:02:01,934 --> 00:02:04,810
I want to ask you bluntly.

30
00:02:04,811 --> 00:02:06,570
If I say it's too late, are you going to not do it?

31
00:02:07,849 --> 00:02:16,131
Whether you're in your teens, twenties, thirties, or forties, if there's something you want to do, you just do it.

32
00:02:16,415 --> 00:02:19,930
Even if it doesn't work out, leaving it as a hobby is good too, isn't it?

33
00:02:19,931 --> 00:02:24,410
Actually, in your teens and twenties, it's never too late to start anything.

34
00:02:24,411 --> 00:02:27,050
You shouldn't ask this kind of question.

35
00:02:27,051 --> 00:02:30,490
But once you get to your thirties, it does get a little worrying.

36
00:02:30,491 --> 00:02:36,170
In my thirties, I'm XX years old. Is it too late to start drawing?

37
00:02:36,230 --> 00:02:43,990
What that question really means is, "If I start studying here and it doesn't work out, I'll have wasted a few years, so should I really start?"

38
00:02:43,991 --> 00:02:46,230
That's what they're asking.

39
00:02:46,231 --> 00:02:48,310
It's when you're afraid of making a new start.

40
00:02:49,885 --> 00:02:56,961
I know an older brother who started drawing in his early thirties and now draws really, really well.

41
00:02:58,225 --> 00:03:00,730
His mindset was clear and he was full of drive.

42
00:03:02,119 --> 00:03:04,410
Those kinds of people wouldn't even have asked the question.

43
00:03:05,690 --> 00:03:09,660
From your mid-to-late thirties, it's really worrying.

44
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:18,821
I think these people currently have a job, but they love drawing so much that they want to change careers.

45
00:03:19,984 --> 00:03:23,251
If you invest your time left after work and study, that's plenty.

46
00:03:24,215 --> 00:03:28,880
It's already late, and it can't be any earlier than now, so put aside the shame.

47
00:03:30,329 --> 00:03:33,900
It's time to put it down.

48
00:03:33,901 --> 00:03:35,420
So what if you try it and it doesn't work out?

49
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:38,700
Work hard at your current job and keep drawing as a hobby.

50
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:46,220
Wouldn't that be fine?

51
00:03:46,221 --> 00:03:49,180
Those who are doing nothing and suddenly think, "Oh, I might as well try drawing."

52
00:03:50,810 --> 00:03:56,260
I'm sorry, but that's a stretch.

53
00:03:56,945 --> 00:03:58,650
Drawing isn't that easy.

54
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:05,930
There are already so many people who are excellent, talented, and motivated.

55
00:04:05,931 --> 00:04:08,250
Can you compete with those people?

56
00:04:10,015 --> 00:04:14,570
If you think it's too late to start drawing, then everything else is late too.

57
00:04:14,571 --> 00:04:17,290
You can't start anything.

58
00:04:17,291 --> 00:04:23,610
I think drawing is the art form least affected by aging compared to other disciplines.

59
00:04:23,611 --> 00:04:24,611
Start.

60
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:30,910
There are always people who ask, "If I try it and it doesn't work out, will you take responsibility?"

61
00:04:30,911 --> 00:04:32,190
Why would I?

62
00:04:32,191 --> 00:04:34,830
You're responsible for your own life.

63
00:04:34,910 --> 00:04:35,910
That's a given.

64
00:04:37,469 --> 00:04:42,000
I'm a complete beginner. What should I draw first?

65
00:04:42,001 --> 00:04:48,041
I thought a mid-level class like this wouldn't have these worries, but surprisingly, they do.

66
00:04:48,325 --> 00:04:58,120
If there's nothing to draw, I recommend copy-study, and in fact some people find copy-study difficult so they can't do it.

67
00:04:58,121 --> 00:05:04,081
They don't know how to go about it, and just drawing cubes and cylinders is too boring.

68
00:05:04,860 --> 00:05:09,031
So I recommend drawing your own left hand.

69
00:05:09,910 --> 00:05:13,480
If you're left-handed, draw your right hand.

70
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:17,960
I recommend line drawing, but the method is whatever works.

71
00:05:17,961 --> 00:05:26,600
It's one of the most complex structures in the human body, yet people tend to think of it as lower in importance than the face.

72
00:05:26,601 --> 00:05:35,960
Since it's made up of various shapes like cylinders, spheres, and cubes, there's no better practice than this.

73
00:05:35,961 --> 00:05:39,681
No matter how well you draw, if the hand is clumsy, you lose points.

74
00:05:40,410 --> 00:05:44,780
Practice with the mindset of mastering your hand.

75
00:05:44,781 --> 00:05:46,860
Try drawing it using basic shapes.

76
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:51,710
Your hand and your ability to see form will improve.

77
00:05:51,711 --> 00:05:55,470
And don't take a photo; draw while looking at your own hand.

78
00:05:55,471 --> 00:05:57,470
Your eye for seeing in 3D will improve.

79
00:06:00,219 --> 00:06:03,630
How much should I draw per day?

80
00:06:03,631 --> 00:06:10,750
These days, counting just the time I actually hold my pen and draw, I draw about five to six hours a day.

81
00:06:11,125 --> 00:06:14,471
I sit for twelve hours because I'm distracted a lot.

82
00:06:16,150 --> 00:06:17,150
That's because this is my job.

83
00:06:18,285 --> 00:06:22,001
The longer your drawing career gets, the higher your eye becomes.

84
00:06:22,810 --> 00:06:27,301
But since your hand can't keep up, it seems like you end up drawing more.

85
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:35,640
Back when I was preparing for jobs, my eye was lower, so I was satisfied drawing just two to three hours.

86
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:38,520
I'd think, "That's probably enough," and stop.

87
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:43,260
And I'd rest for ten days after finishing a picture.

88
00:06:43,261 --> 00:06:45,180
The saying "a frog in a well" fits perfectly.

89
00:06:46,435 --> 00:06:49,840
Everyone, interact with others a lot.

90
00:06:49,841 --> 00:06:55,840
Even join a drawing group chat, exchange opinions, and sometimes get inspired.

91
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:01,770
If you compare yourselves and feel your own shortcomings, that's a great opportunity.

92
00:07:02,945 --> 00:07:06,700
While you make up for them, your skill also grows.

93
00:07:08,995 --> 00:07:12,830
You'll end up drawing longer in order to compensate.

94
00:07:15,130 --> 00:07:19,660
Don't hold yourself to a fixed number of hours; draw based on need.

95
00:07:22,405 --> 00:07:26,400
Drawing shouldn't become daily homework.

96
00:07:30,865 --> 00:07:34,890
My drawing is gradually resembling the style of an artist I like. How should I go about it?

97
00:07:36,010 --> 00:07:44,970
If there's an artist you really like, collect their drawings and look at them every day, and it's natural that you'll start to resemble it.

98
00:07:44,971 --> 00:07:47,850
They say a drawing resembles its creator's face.

99
00:07:47,851 --> 00:07:49,370
Of course, it'll be a lot romanticized, but you'll resemble them.

100
00:07:50,095 --> 00:07:55,290
Anyway, if you look at them frequently, they become familiar to your eye and you unconsciously draw similarly.

101
00:07:55,291 --> 00:07:57,850
I said it's natural.

102
00:07:57,851 --> 00:07:59,450
Naturally growing to resemble it.

103
00:07:59,745 --> 00:08:02,490
I think that's not bad.

104
00:08:02,491 --> 00:08:08,090
It's like a child being cared for and nourished by parents, growing up, then becoming independent.

105
00:08:08,091 --> 00:08:11,050
In the end, they go off to live their own life.

106
00:08:11,051 --> 00:08:12,410
Drawing is exactly the same.

107
00:08:12,411 --> 00:08:21,571
Once your skill grows and you become a professional and do work, the degree of dependence on that artist decreases, and your own style will naturally emerge.

108
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:28,330
If after ten or twenty years you still can't become independent, well, that's a bit sad.

109
00:08:29,875 --> 00:08:33,900
What about using a grid when doing copy-study?

110
00:08:33,901 --> 00:08:35,419
Everyone, you know what a grid is, right?

111
00:08:35,695 --> 00:08:37,500
You draw a grid like this.

112
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:49,370
So you draw a grid on top of a photo, and then copy the grid over, and you draw exactly the same thing matching the grid.

113
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,210
This makes it easy to draw the same shape when doing copy-study.

114
00:09:00,470 --> 00:09:04,600
If your goal is to draw exactly the same, then sure, that's fine.

115
00:09:04,601 --> 00:09:11,640
But if it's for study, you're doing copy-study to build your observational skills, so why are you doing something like this?

116
00:09:11,641 --> 00:09:15,160
It's no different from solving a problem while looking at the answer key.

117
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:16,240
Not good.

118
00:09:17,205 --> 00:09:22,110
Don't do this; instead, create an imaginary grid inside your head.

119
00:09:22,111 --> 00:09:23,470
Don't try to make it easy.

120
00:09:26,350 --> 00:09:29,230
I spend too much time fixing a drawing.

121
00:09:29,231 --> 00:09:30,231
What should I do?

122
00:09:31,350 --> 00:09:34,120
Fixing drawings is a very common thing.

123
00:09:34,121 --> 00:09:36,520
Everyone makes corrections.

124
00:09:36,521 --> 00:09:40,920
But there are cases where it's so time-consuming that it's exhausting.

125
00:09:40,921 --> 00:09:47,960
This is in line with what I talked about in Chapter 1. Just don't keep drawing.

126
00:09:47,961 --> 00:09:59,320
If you keep looking at only that one picture, you can't get out of the whirlpool of doubting whether you're doing well or not, or whether you drew it correctly.

127
00:09:59,321 --> 00:10:00,321
Don't draw it.

128
00:10:00,655 --> 00:10:02,730
Draw something else.

129
00:10:02,731 --> 00:10:05,850
That's why I try to work on multiple drawings at once.

130
00:10:07,265 --> 00:10:10,380
It means to set it aside and look at it with some time in between.

131
00:10:10,381 --> 00:10:14,860
If you work at a company, you can naturally set it aside and look at it over time.

132
00:10:14,861 --> 00:10:19,500
Because you do company art at the company and personal work at home.

133
00:10:19,501 --> 00:10:25,100
The case where you have to keep holding onto it is when your progress plan is firm.

134
00:10:25,101 --> 00:10:29,381
If you're stuck, put it down for a while and look at it after some time. That's good.

135
00:10:32,495 --> 00:10:36,280
Please tell me how to grow my social media followers.

136
00:10:36,281 --> 00:10:39,720
There are a lot of people who are active on social media.

137
00:10:39,721 --> 00:10:41,880
I'm active too.

138
00:10:41,881 --> 00:10:46,280
Up until a year or two ago, I had no interest in growing followers.

139
00:10:46,281 --> 00:10:52,360
These days I feel that followers equal money, so I'm working hard to grow them.

140
00:10:52,361 --> 00:10:55,160
Especially since I became a freelancer.

141
00:10:55,161 --> 00:10:58,040
If you're a freelancer, I strongly recommend being active.

142
00:10:58,790 --> 00:11:01,720
For those of us who draw, this doesn't have to be a waste of life.

143
00:11:01,740 --> 00:11:02,740
It can be worthwhile.

144
00:11:03,850 --> 00:11:09,370
The way to grow them is to draw well.

145
00:11:09,371 --> 00:11:11,450
Not technically, but attractively.

146
00:11:12,670 --> 00:11:16,201
With drawing that can appeal to the masses.

147
00:11:16,840 --> 00:11:19,240
The masses are honest.

148
00:11:19,241 --> 00:11:22,760
They follow people who draw the kind of pictures they want to keep seeing.

149
00:11:23,990 --> 00:11:28,920
So the best method is to draw fan art of popular anime or movies.

150
00:11:30,595 --> 00:11:34,470
If you draw it well, you can absorb that fandom.

151
00:11:35,770 --> 00:11:39,740
Is it embarrassing to draw fan art of something unpopular?

152
00:11:39,741 --> 00:11:45,500
If so, then you have to build a fan base comparable to a fandom in your own personal way.

153
00:11:48,895 --> 00:11:49,895
That's hard.

154
00:11:50,470 --> 00:11:55,210
You could draw provocative, sexy drawings, but I won't recommend that.

155
00:11:55,211 --> 00:11:58,490
Honestly, I don't know of another good method.

156
00:11:58,491 --> 00:12:04,490
I'm not going to give you the cliche answer that if you draw hard, followers will grow.

157
00:12:04,491 --> 00:12:09,050
But I can tell you the characteristics of each social media platform.

158
00:12:09,051 --> 00:12:13,011
Let me look mainly at the ones I've used.

159
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:17,819
Facebook is the first social media I started.

160
00:12:18,270 --> 00:12:24,350
Compared to casual drawings, realistic drawings seem to get a better response here.

161
00:12:24,351 --> 00:12:30,311
This isn't a structure where followers grow explosively, so you need to post pictures with patience.

162
00:12:31,670 --> 00:12:36,440
Compared to other platforms, it feels like the drawing population here is the smallest.

163
00:12:37,295 --> 00:12:39,960
And the image quality degradation is a downside.

164
00:12:41,720 --> 00:12:45,491
On Instagram, relatively diverse styles of drawings are posted.

165
00:12:47,070 --> 00:12:57,001
However, because of the square frame structure, it's not suitable for posting concept art, and cropped upper-body or face drawings work effectively.

166
00:12:57,685 --> 00:12:59,710
So I didn't use it much.

167
00:13:00,890 --> 00:13:06,750
And compared to Facebook, if you catch the flow well, it's a structure where followers can grow quickly.

168
00:13:08,110 --> 00:13:15,070
In my experience, on Instagram the key is posting consistently without taking long breaks.

169
00:13:15,150 --> 00:13:23,230
It's case by case, but I feel that five decent drawings bring in many more followers than one great drawing.

170
00:13:26,035 --> 00:13:30,460
Twitter is a place where casual-style drawings are strong.

171
00:13:30,461 --> 00:13:35,260
Still, realistic and semi-realistic drawings aren't bad to post either.

172
00:13:35,261 --> 00:13:39,620
Twitter here really seems like an honest platform.

173
00:13:39,990 --> 00:13:43,441
Followers can also grow explosively depending on the situation.

174
00:13:44,325 --> 00:13:47,360
It feels like it has the largest user population.

175
00:13:48,350 --> 00:13:52,560
And even if you have many followers, you might not get likes.

176
00:13:52,561 --> 00:13:59,401
I started about a year ago, and it seems like a very provocative, honest, and fun platform.

177
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:11,080
And ArtStation. On ArtStation, casual drawings don't do well, while realistic-style or Western-style drawings are extremely strong.

178
00:14:11,081 --> 00:14:17,521
If you draw semi-realistic or realistic style like us, it's a place you can go often to appreciate and upload.

179
00:14:18,490 --> 00:14:21,340
A lot of commission inquiries come in here.

180
00:14:21,341 --> 00:14:23,580
They come in English, so it's a bit scary.

181
00:14:23,581 --> 00:14:27,900
Anyway, here too, you have to post consistently for followers to grow.

182
00:14:27,980 --> 00:14:32,380
It's a place that grows slowly, so you need to act with patience.

183
00:14:37,270 --> 00:14:39,880
So far we've had a Q&A session.

184
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:46,360
From now on, let me take some time to give feedback on a few pictures that class members have posted.

185
00:14:46,361 --> 00:14:49,880
It doesn't feel great because I'm revealing public work, to be honest.

186
00:14:49,881 --> 00:14:55,601
But you all must have submitted a consent form to use this as a feedback video, so don't feel too wronged.

187
00:14:56,410 --> 00:14:59,740
First, this is Yoon Bong-woo's drawing.

188
00:14:59,820 --> 00:15:08,220
This is an event page where you write your own strengths and areas to improve, but they asked for feedback, which caught me off guard, but I'll do it.

189
00:15:10,115 --> 00:15:12,670
First, let me examine the picture.

190
00:15:12,830 --> 00:15:16,350
I can clearly tell you're aiming for a realistic style.

191
00:15:16,351 --> 00:15:20,110
The face seems to have been done referencing a photo.

192
00:15:20,111 --> 00:15:22,110
You really put in hard work on the rendering.

193
00:15:22,485 --> 00:15:26,430
The effort you put into finishing it is evident.

194
00:15:26,431 --> 00:15:27,630
Let me give you a round of applause.

195
00:15:28,595 --> 00:15:32,590
That aside, this picture has many problems.

196
00:15:32,591 --> 00:15:39,950
This picture was drawn before you took the class, so you, the person who drew it, of course, and all of you who took the class.

197
00:15:40,090 --> 00:15:43,571
Will be able to point out the problems.

198
00:15:44,250 --> 00:15:47,260
The first issue is that the figure is quite off.

199
00:15:48,060 --> 00:15:53,180
When drawing, I always recommend taking the pose yourself and then drawing.

200
00:15:53,181 --> 00:15:56,540
We learned that the center of gravity and the sense of movement are important.

201
00:15:56,541 --> 00:15:58,700
Doesn't this leg look like it's floating?

202
00:16:00,860 --> 00:16:03,310
Let me look at it while covering up the lower body.

203
00:16:05,789 --> 00:16:09,591
It's an upper body that seems like it should be standing straight to look right.

204
00:16:10,120 --> 00:16:16,200
Conversely, let me cover the upper body.

205
00:16:16,201 --> 00:16:17,960
The pelvis is pushed way back.

206
00:16:17,961 --> 00:16:20,680
What kind of pose is this?

207
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:23,560
Would this look a bit more natural?

208
00:16:23,561 --> 00:16:24,760
Saying it's leaning on one leg isn't quite it.

209
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:31,680
The pelvis and the floor are parallel, so it looks awkward.

210
00:16:31,681 --> 00:16:35,160
Second, you divided the value steps too much.

211
00:16:36,380 --> 00:16:44,551
Bright, medium bright, medium dark, very dark. It's divided up in such detail like that.

212
00:16:45,905 --> 00:16:50,330
But here, the shoes and the hair are done well.

213
00:16:50,331 --> 00:16:53,690
It would be good to also divide the inherent value a bit.

214
00:16:53,691 --> 00:16:59,050
Besides that, there are issues with the design and form.

215
00:16:59,051 --> 00:17:06,890
The biggest problem is that even though you couldn't draw the form properly first, you tried to render it like a photo anyway.

216
00:17:06,891 --> 00:17:11,450
There really are a lot of people who aim for realism and draw like this.

217
00:17:11,451 --> 00:17:12,810
Form comes first, everyone.

218
00:17:14,964 --> 00:17:17,630
I changed it like this.

219
00:17:18,429 --> 00:17:27,710
Since I thought the thing this person needs most is the ability to form shapes, I left out all the detail and drew with the form as the focus.

220
00:17:27,711 --> 00:17:31,191
And I clearly divided the inherent value.

221
00:17:33,629 --> 00:17:37,840
This part where the leg is stretched out is meant to show leaning on one leg, right?

222
00:17:38,719 --> 00:17:41,881
I changed it to a plain lean-on-one-leg pose.

223
00:17:46,229 --> 00:17:49,720
The biggest thing I changed is this armor part.

224
00:17:49,721 --> 00:17:51,560
Overall it's too thin.

225
00:17:51,561 --> 00:17:54,120
And the form is quite off too.

226
00:17:54,200 --> 00:17:59,000
It's good to think of everything as a combination of basic shapes and draw accordingly.

227
00:17:59,001 --> 00:18:06,040
Take this shoulder for example. It's the joint area, a continuous set of cylinders.

228
00:18:07,669 --> 00:18:10,040
The other parts are the same too.

229
00:18:10,040 --> 00:18:16,600
And I expressed the shadow like this to show the height differences of the armor.

230
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:20,760
The whole thing is clinging to the body, so it looks like there's no sense of thickness at all.

231
00:18:22,779 --> 00:18:24,510
You're also drawing the shading wrong.

232
00:18:24,539 --> 00:18:30,711
You need to practice drawing the shading simply rather than over-dividing the value steps like this.

233
00:18:31,129 --> 00:18:35,381
Drawing the lower-body armor like this makes the picture look flat.

234
00:18:37,450 --> 00:18:45,450
Imagine what it looks like from the side.

235
00:18:45,789 --> 00:18:46,890
Having these curves

236
00:18:49,054 --> 00:18:50,170
would be natural.

237
00:18:50,889 --> 00:18:55,571
If there are curves, the shading will change like this too.

238
00:18:56,359 --> 00:19:01,800
The armor's scratches are also too artificial and all over.

239
00:19:01,801 --> 00:19:04,440
You really got cut evenly by the sword all over.

240
00:19:04,441 --> 00:19:09,080
Even setting that aside, I said you shouldn't give density to the whole thing.

241
00:19:09,081 --> 00:19:11,880
You shouldn't try to cover up a lack of form-ability with coloring.

242
00:19:11,880 --> 00:19:14,881
You need to practice line drawing more.

243
00:19:15,364 --> 00:19:21,241
You seem like someone with weight behind you, so if you just slightly turn your study direction, I think you'll grow quickly.

244
00:19:24,009 --> 00:19:28,250
The second drawing is by a person named Chok-chok-han Choko-chip.

245
00:19:28,251 --> 00:19:31,050
You drew it with a nice atmosphere.

246
00:19:31,050 --> 00:19:33,370
The classic expression was good.

247
00:19:33,371 --> 00:19:37,370
This person said they were worried about a common design.

248
00:19:37,371 --> 00:19:39,610
I emphasized the power of ordinariness.

249
00:19:40,044 --> 00:19:43,770
If you apply that, the design itself isn't bad.

250
00:19:43,771 --> 00:19:47,171
But it seems like you could bring out the charm elements a bit.

251
00:19:48,189 --> 00:19:53,120
So I fixed this picture like this.

252
00:19:53,120 --> 00:19:55,120
I stabilized the pose.

253
00:19:55,334 --> 00:19:58,841
I extended the skirt hem to secure volume.

254
00:20:01,074 --> 00:20:06,220
In terms of design, first I fixed the shoulder strap shape.

255
00:20:08,729 --> 00:20:13,020
I added a subtle pattern without harming the original.

256
00:20:13,020 --> 00:20:17,980
If the design is too simple, you can increase density with patterns like this.

257
00:20:17,981 --> 00:20:22,009
But you have to be careful that the pattern doesn't break the armor's form.

258
00:20:22,865 --> 00:20:25,420
Also, I exaggerated the elbow armor.

259
00:20:28,149 --> 00:20:30,840
You said you wanted to make a convincing design.

260
00:20:30,889 --> 00:20:33,400
If so, you have to show something first.

261
00:20:34,884 --> 00:20:38,400
If there's nothing here like this, there's nothing to convince with.

262
00:20:38,401 --> 00:20:39,401
Right?

263
00:20:41,399 --> 00:20:48,730
Overall it's bland, but seeing that you put a pattern on the thigh, I thought maybe this is the point.

264
00:20:48,730 --> 00:20:55,530
To bring out the point, I drew a guard piece and moved the pattern there.

265
00:20:55,531 --> 00:20:58,810
Point elements have to be clearly visible.

266
00:20:58,811 --> 00:21:05,900
Likewise, I changed the design of the bland weapon a bit.

267
00:21:06,914 --> 00:21:08,700
Using this ring in the background, along with.

268
00:21:11,154 --> 00:21:15,900
The patterns on the armor and so on, I brought out continuity in the design.

269
00:21:20,919 --> 00:21:24,970
The lower body armor is unusual compared to other parts.

270
00:21:24,971 --> 00:21:30,010
Since it's a piece of armor that breaks the norm, I judged that it could also be a charm element.

271
00:21:32,464 --> 00:21:34,330
You have to exaggerate charm elements.

272
00:21:35,449 --> 00:21:38,851
And I unified the overall flow.

273
00:21:46,305 --> 00:21:47,305
Like this.

274
00:21:50,675 --> 00:21:55,660
I followed the flow that arises from this weapon crossing the whole picture diagonally.

275
00:21:57,080 --> 00:22:01,171
Let me go over the method of unifying this flow with my own drawing.

276
00:22:03,204 --> 00:22:04,800
You can unify the flow like this.

277
00:22:10,029 --> 00:22:11,360
This drawing is the same too.

278
00:22:14,360 --> 00:22:20,090
And this drawing also unifies the flow.

279
00:22:20,334 --> 00:22:22,490
This could be a kind of tip.

280
00:22:22,491 --> 00:22:25,731
This is really a golden tip, so I'll reveal it only to you.

281
00:22:27,819 --> 00:22:31,610
The third one is Park Seong-ho's drawing.

282
00:22:31,611 --> 00:22:35,290
You drew it in a sheet format with front and back sides.

283
00:22:35,291 --> 00:22:36,810
Good.

284
00:22:36,811 --> 00:22:40,410
It was the most concept-art-like of the three.

285
00:22:40,411 --> 00:22:43,130
The design is simple and all fine.

286
00:22:43,131 --> 00:22:49,210
You used accent colors appropriately, and it's good that it flows simply downward.

287
00:22:49,211 --> 00:22:53,370
But as you can see now, the figure and form are quite off.

288
00:22:53,371 --> 00:22:55,610
And this character has its own charm.

289
00:22:55,949 --> 00:22:58,030
It seems like it wasn't brought out properly.

290
00:22:59,279 --> 00:23:02,320
I changed it like this.

291
00:23:02,321 --> 00:23:07,920
I barely touched the color and design, and focused on fixing the form.

292
00:23:07,921 --> 00:23:14,080
So I thought this character is a woman with a sturdy physique who does boxing or close combat.

293
00:23:14,855 --> 00:23:16,800
So I brought out the muscles.

294
00:23:17,954 --> 00:23:21,390
It's great that you designed the clothing so the back muscles can be seen.

295
00:23:22,870 --> 00:23:28,440
The figure as a whole is quite disappointing, so it's hard to point out each part individually.

296
00:23:28,441 --> 00:23:30,520
It looks like you need a lot of figure study.

297
00:23:32,704 --> 00:23:38,220
Looking at a bit of it. I said it's good to lower the shoulder on the side where the pelvis is raised.

298
00:23:43,069 --> 00:23:47,120
Actually, the torso stays still like this while only the shoulder goes up,

299
00:23:48,544 --> 00:23:51,900
it's not about the concept of lowering.

300
00:23:51,901 --> 00:23:52,901
The waist bends.

301
00:24:07,909 --> 00:24:08,910
The body and shoulder together.

302
00:24:08,911 --> 00:24:13,790
And the body is too flat.

303
00:24:13,949 --> 00:24:15,390
There's no curvature.

304
00:24:15,390 --> 00:24:20,030
This is a case of paying attention only to the chest curvature but not the body curvature.

305
00:24:20,031 --> 00:24:22,590
Let me explain while looking at the side view.

306
00:24:23,294 --> 00:24:25,450
Since you drew the pose with the waist pulled back a bit,

307
00:24:27,100 --> 00:24:31,191
the lower abdomen area should be drawn as if it goes in toward the back like this.

308
00:24:32,194 --> 00:24:35,910
And draw the shading considering this ribcage line.

309
00:24:40,225 --> 00:24:41,225
Like this.

310
00:24:44,619 --> 00:24:48,101
And I've been emphasizing the importance of the first-pass coloring.

311
00:24:50,114 --> 00:24:53,511
Accessories like belts that cover the body's silhouette

312
00:24:54,549 --> 00:24:59,070
should be drawn after the form is made clear through the first-pass coloring.

313
00:25:02,864 --> 00:25:12,051
And while the completion here is a bit lacking, it's good that you tried to describe the invisible parts and explain something of this character, even if I don't know exactly what.

314
00:25:12,649 --> 00:25:14,890
It can be said to be a good example of concept art.

315
00:25:18,064 --> 00:25:21,690
It's also good that you designed the boots by bringing out that subtle angle.

316
00:25:24,034 --> 00:25:29,150
If you just build up your drawing skill, you'll be a really good concept artist.

317
00:25:30,564 --> 00:25:34,000
The lecture has reached its final stage before we know it.

318
00:25:34,080 --> 00:25:35,600
How was it?

319
00:25:35,601 --> 00:25:41,040
There were rough spots, but I tried to teach you as much as I could within what I know.

320
00:25:41,040 --> 00:25:47,321
If you accept these as things you'll recall once in a while while drawing in the future, I'd be really glad.

321
00:25:48,734 --> 00:25:53,850
This time we had a Q&A and feedback session.

322
00:25:53,851 --> 00:25:55,610
I'll see you in the outro.

323
00:25:55,611 --> 00:25:56,611
Great work, everyone.
